window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_physics_en"][39]={"university_physics_en:M39-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M39-L1 – Brownian Motion and Stokes–Einstein Diffusion<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M13: statistical mechanics; M6:\nlaminar flow; M38 · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M38&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Connect mean-square\ndisplacement, diffusivity and hydrodynamic friction for an idealized\ncolloidal sphere.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Thermal molecular collisions lead to irregular motion of microscopic\nparticles. In free homogeneous diffusion at long times, ensemble\nmean-square displacement grows linearly in time, while the mean signed\ndisplacement vanishes. This is an ensemble statement, not a\ndeterministic trajectory for one particle.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>In one dimension, the diffusion equation with constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand vanishing boundary flux gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle[x(t)-x(0)]^2\\rangle=2Dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a sphere of hydrodynamic radius\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin a Newtonian continuum fluid with no-slip boundary and small Reynolds\nnumber,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ζ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>6<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\zeta=6\\pi\\eta r_h<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the Einstein relation yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ζ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=k_BT/\\zeta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe hydrodynamic and geometric radii need not coincide. The overdamped\nmodel omits the short-time ballistic regime.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mn>6<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mi>η<\/mi><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">1D<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=\\frac{k_BT}{6\\pi\\eta r_h},\\qquad\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle=2Dt\\quad(\\text{1D}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Choose illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>300<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T=300\\,\\mathrm K<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=10^{-3}\\,\\mathrm{Pa\\,s}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r<\/mi><mi>h<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r_h=1\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUsing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.380649<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>23<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k_B=1.380649\\times10^{-23}\\,\\mathrm{J\\,K^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.20<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>13<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.220<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\approx2.20\\times10^{-13}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}}=0.220\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAfter\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t=10\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\none-dimensional RMS displacement is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{2Dt}\\approx2.10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot directed distance. The figure shows the ensemble trend.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_033_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Linear growth of one-dimensional mean-square displacement over time in an illustrative Brownian model.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Linear growth of one-dimensional\nmean-square displacement over time in an illustrative Brownian\nmodel.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Ensemble illustration, not measured particle tracks.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when hydrodynamic radius doubles at fixed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>η<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T,\\eta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle\\Delta x^2\\rangle<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nafter five seconds in the numerical example?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why cannot a single trajectory replace an ensemble mean?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2Dt\\approx2.20\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Individual paths fluctuate; expected squared displacement is a\nstatistical average.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Stokes–Einstein may fail near interfaces, under confinement, in\nviscoelastic media, at high concentration or for nonequilibrium active\nparticles. Dimensionality matters: in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent spatial dimensions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒓<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>d<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle|\\Delta\\mathbf r|^2\\rangle=2dDt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot always\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2Dt<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The statistical description leads to Fick transport and coupled\nreaction–diffusion. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-430j-fields-forces-and-flows-in-biological-systems-fall-2015/pages/calendar/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Fields, Forces, and Flows in Biological Systems (Calendar)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic coverage for Stokes–Einstein, Fick, reaction–diffusion and Péclet number; not an independent formula check; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L1 – Brownian Motion and Stokes–Einstein Diffusion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-72-non-equilibrium-statistical-mechanics-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Non-Equilibrium Statistical Mechanics: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic index for Brownian motion, stochastic processes and the Fokker–Planck equation; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L1 – Brownian Motion and Stokes–Einstein Diffusion · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M39-L2 – Fickian Diffusion, Reactions and the Damköhler Number<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M39-L1; M31: differential equations\nand numerical boundary problems · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Combine flux and conservation\nfor first-order consumption and interpret the reaction penetration\nlength.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In tissues or catalytic membranes, species diffuse while undergoing\nchemical conversion. Their spatial concentration obeys a local\nconservation law. A unique concentration profile requires an initial\ncondition, boundary conditions and a reaction law.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Fick's first law is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J=-D\\nabla c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nCombining\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mo>⋅<\/mo><mspace width=\"-0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tc=-\\nabla\\!\\cdot\\!\\mathbf J-kc<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith uniform\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>c<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tc=D\\nabla^2c-kc<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the stationary one-dimensional half-space\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(0)=c_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>∞<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(\\infty)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe solution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c(x)=c_0\\exp(-x/\\ell)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=\\sqrt{D/k}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe dimensionless Damköhler number\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Da}=kL^2/D=(L/\\ell)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncompares diffusion and reaction timescales.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>c<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mfrac><mi>D<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mfrac><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><mi>D<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\frac{\\partial c}{\\partial t}=D\\nabla^2c-kc,\\qquad\\ell=\\sqrt{\\frac Dk},\\quad\\mathrm{Da}=\\frac{kL^2}{D}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For a constructed medium choose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>k<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k=0.01\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.20<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=0.20\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell=0.10\\,\\mathrm{mm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Da}=4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>c<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>c<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.368<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c/c_0=e^{-1}\\approx0.368<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the stationary exponential profile and its penetration\nlength.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_095_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Exponential steady concentration decay with penetration length and normalized concentration indicated.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Exponential steady concentration decay\nwith penetration length and normalized concentration\nindicated.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic one-species linear model in a half-space.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when reaction rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbecomes four times larger?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the relative concentration at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What else is needed for a time-dependent solution?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Penetration length is halved.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.135<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">e^{-2}\\approx0.135<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Initial concentration profile and boundary conditions.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The exponential solution changes for finite layers with different\nboundary conditions, saturable enzyme kinetics, spatially varying\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nor appreciable advection. The stated\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Da}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nuses first-order kinetics; other definitions are used for different\nreaction models.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Reaction rates can create feedback in biological networks. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-430j-fields-forces-and-flows-in-biological-systems-fall-2015/pages/calendar/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Fields, Forces, and Flows in Biological Systems (Calendar)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic coverage for Stokes–Einstein, Fick, reaction–diffusion and Péclet number; not an independent formula check; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L2 – Fickian Diffusion, Reactions and the Damköhler Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-430j-fields-forces-and-flows-in-biological-systems-fall-2015/resources/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Fields, Forces, and Flows: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lists instructional materials on reaction, diffusion and transport; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L2 – Fickian Diffusion, Reactions and the Damköhler Number · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M39-L3 – Biological Feedback Circuits, Hill Functions and Stability<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M39-L2; M31: ordinary differential\nequations; M13: stochastic processes · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate an autoregulated\nproduction–decay model, find a fixed point and test local stability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Cellular regulatory networks couple gene expression, signalling and\nmetabolism. In simple negative autoregulation, increasing protein\nabundance reduces its production. This can stabilize a steady operating\nlevel; multi-component networks may also oscillate or show richer\ndynamics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>Use the illustrative concentration dynamics\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=\\alpha/[1+(x/K)^n]-\\gamma x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha,K,\\gamma&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthe production rate decreases monotonically. Fixed points satisfy\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f(x^*)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nlocal stability of a scalar ODE follows from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x^*)&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSince\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>α<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(x)=-\\alpha/[K(1+x/K)^2]-\\gamma&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthere is at most one positive fixed point and it is stable. Negative\nsingle-component feedback alone does not imply bistability.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>α<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>K<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mo>−<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mo>⇒<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.222em\"><\/mspace><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">locally stable<\/mtext><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=\\frac{\\alpha}{1+(x/K)}-\\gamma x,\\qquad f^{\\prime}(x^*)&lt;0\\ \\Rightarrow\\ \\text{locally stable}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>With constructed dimensionless\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=2/(1+x)-x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe fixed-point equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x(1+x)=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x^*=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the other algebraic root\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis physically inadmissible). The slope is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mi>′<\/mi><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^{\\prime}(1)=-2/4-1=-1.5&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe teaching figure shows production and decay rates intersecting at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_021_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Declining regulated production and rising linear decay, with a stable intersection at concentration one.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Declining regulated production and rising\nlinear decay, with a stable intersection at concentration\none.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Dimensionless one-protein model; no experimental biological\ndata.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the production rate at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the sign of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Is a negative concentration fixed point physically admissible?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Production is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>2<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the chosen units.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>+<\/mi><mn>0.833<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=2/1.5-0.5\\approx+0.833<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nincreases.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No, concentrations are nonnegative.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This is a continuous deterministic one-variable model with a fixed\nproduction and linear decay law. Real cells have finite molecule\nnumbers, intrinsic noise, delays and spatial structure. Multidimensional\nsystems require eigenvalue or other stability analyses.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Nonlinear couplings can produce much richer temporal patterns. <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-591j-systems-biology-fall-2014/pages/readings/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Systems Biology: Readings<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topics on autoregulatory biological networks and stability; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L3 – Biological Feedback Circuits, Hill Functions and Stability · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-591j-systems-biology-fall-2014/resources/autoregulation-feedback-and-bistability/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Autoregulation, Feedback and Bistability<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture resource addressing positive and negative feedback; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L3 – Biological Feedback Circuits, Hill Functions and Stability · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M39-L4 – Nonlinear Dynamics, the Lorenz System and Lyapunov Exponents<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M39-L3; M31: numerical integration;\nM19: dynamical systems · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish local trajectory\nstretching from global chaos and identify Lorenz equilibria\ncorrectly.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Nonlinear equations may amplify small differences in initial\nconditions. The Lorenz model originated as a drastic simplification of\nconvective flow equations. Its three-dimensional trajectory is a\nmathematical teaching model, not measured weather. A positive asymptotic\nmaximal Lyapunov exponent signals exponential sensitivity; one short\nepisode of separation alone does not establish chaos.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The dimensionless model reads\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=\\sigma(y-x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot y=x(\\rho-z)-y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot z=xy-\\beta z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith positive parameters. At the origin, the linearized\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndynamics form a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mo>×<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\times2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmatrix. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nits determinant is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma(1-\\rho)&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the origin is unstable. Two further equilibria occur at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\pm\\sqrt{\\beta(\\rho-1)},\\pm\\sqrt{\\beta(\\rho-1)},\\rho-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTheir stability depends on parameters; their mere existence does not\nimply chaos.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>x<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>y<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"1.0em\"><\/mspace><mover><mi>z<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̇<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mi>y<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>β<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\dot x=\\sigma(y-x),\\quad\\dot y=x(\\rho-z)-y,\\quad\\dot z=xy-\\beta z.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=8/3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=28<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe two nonzero equilibria are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mn>72<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><msqrt><mn>72<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>,<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>8.485<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>±<\/mi><mn>8.485<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\pm\\sqrt{72},\\pm\\sqrt{72},27)\\approx(\\pm8.485,\\pm8.485,27)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt the origin, the\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nblock has determinant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>270<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-270&lt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand eigenvalues of opposite sign. The figure sketches two possible\nprojected trajectories and marks the equilibria; no Lyapunov exponent is\ninferred from this sketch.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_190_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic x–z projection of loops near two Lorenz equilibria, not traced from measured or integrated data.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic x–z projection of loops near\ntwo Lorenz equilibria, not traced from measured or integrated\ndata.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Schematic projection of Lorenz phase space.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>This is not a numerically integrated trajectory; Lyapunov exponents\nhave not been computed quantitatively.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is the third equilibrium coordinate for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>28<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho=28<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why is the origin unstable for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ρ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\rho&gt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Does temporary growth of a perturbation establish chaos?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>z<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>27<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z^*=27<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">The linear\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>–<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nblock has negative determinant and thus one positive eigenvalue.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">No. Appropriate long-time dynamical analysis is required.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>Lorenz equations are deterministic; measurement noise, numerical\nrounding and finite observation windows affect exponent estimates. The\nplotted phase portrait is a schematic rather than a numerically\nvalidated attractor. Chaos and long-term climate properties of real\nsystems cannot be inferred solely from the three equations.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Energy conversion and dissipation also appear in microscopic\nmolecular motors. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/12-006j-nonlinear-dynamics-chaos-fall-2022/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Nonlinear Dynamics: Chaos, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lecture index for Lorenz equations and Lyapunov exponents; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L4 – Nonlinear Dynamics, the Lorenz System and Lyapunov Exponents · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M39-L5 – Molecular Motors, Chemical Work and Dissipation<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M22: biomolecular energetics; M39-L3:\nregulatory networks · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Compare chemical free energy per\nmodel step with mechanical work and interpret the dissipation sign.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>Molecular motors can transform chemical reaction free energy into\ndirected movement and transport. A recorded step is neither evidence of\nlossless conversion nor proof of one-to-one ATP–step stoichiometry. A\nsimplified energy budget separates available chemical work, load work\nand dissipation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For a hypothetical forward step of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nagainst force\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ntake released chemical free energy as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\mu&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand delivered mechanical work as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{mech}}=Fd<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAn ideal isothermal cycle obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\Delta\\mu-Fd\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor average forward operation. The bound\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>F<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">l<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F_{\\mathrm{stall}}=\\Delta\\mu/d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a reversible limit for strict one-step coupling; competing pathways\nand kinetics can change measured stall forces.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_{\\mathrm{mech}}=Fd,\\qquad T\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{tot}}=\\Delta\\mu-Fd\\ge0.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For deliberately constructed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>80<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\mu=80\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d=8\\,\\mathrm{nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=5\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nmechanical work is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">40\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><msub><mi>S<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>40<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T\\Delta S_{\\mathrm{tot}}=40\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nper ideal cycle. The reversible upper-force bound would be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure represents bookkeeping only; no experimental force curve or\nATP hydrolysis value was copied.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_180_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Energy budget separating chemical release, mechanical load work and dissipation for a constructed motor step.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Energy budget separating chemical\nrelease, mechanical load work and dissipation for a constructed motor\nstep.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Model calculation only; no experimental motor\nparameters.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What load work is obtained for\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">F=4\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat the same\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What would be the reversible upper force bound if step length\ndoubled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>F<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>μ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Fd&lt;\\Delta\\mu<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnot automatically measure an efficiency?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>32<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">32\\,\\mathrm{pN\\,nm}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">5\\,\\mathrm{pN}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the same budget.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Step statistics, chemical coupling and hidden dissipation are not\nestablished.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>This cycle model omits backward steps, futile hydrolysis, variable\ncoupling and fluctuations. The second-law inequality does not determine\na force–velocity curve. Its parameters are not measurements of a\nparticular motor family.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>Fluctuations and friction can be represented by a stochastic equation\nof motion. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/8-592j-statistical-physics-in-biology-spring-2011/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OCW – Statistical Physics in Biology – Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Teaching context on molecular motors; numerical values independently chosen; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L5 – Molecular Motors, Chemical Work and Dissipation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-72-non-equilibrium-statistical-mechanics-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Non-Equilibrium Statistical Mechanics: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic index for Brownian motion, stochastic processes and the Fokker–Planck equation; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L5 – Molecular Motors, Chemical Work and Dissipation · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M39-L6 – Langevin Dynamics, the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Process and Fokker–Planck<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M39-L1: diffusion; M39-L5:\ndissipation; M13: statistics · <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous lesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Relate drift, noise and\nFokker–Planck probability flux consistently and calculate stationary OU\nvariance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>A particle in a harmonic trap is subject to restoring drift and\nthermal kicks. Instead of an individual random trajectory, one can\ndescribe its probability density. Linear drift and additive white\nGaussian forcing define the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process. Its stationary\nwidth must be distinguished carefully between variance and standard\ndeviation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>The Itô equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>d<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">dx=-\\gamma x\\,dt+\\sqrt{2D}\\,dW_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma,D&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives the Fokker–Planck equation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>J<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t p=\\partial_x(\\gamma x p)+D\\partial_x^2p=-\\partial_xJ<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J=-\\gamma xp-D\\partial_xp<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nRequiring vanishing flux at infinite boundaries yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><msup><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_{\\mathrm{st}}(x)\\propto\\exp[-\\gamma x^2/(2D)]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStationary variance equals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Var}(x)=D/\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand standard deviation is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{D/\\gamma}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis assumes constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nno extra drive and a normalizable density.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>γ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mi>D<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_t p=\\partial_x(\\gamma xp)+D\\partial_x^2p,\\qquad\\mathrm{Var}_{\\mathrm{st}}(x)=\\frac D\\gamma.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>Choose illustrative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma=0.5\\,\\mathrm{s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=2\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D/\\gamma=4\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_x=2\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand mean relaxation time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>τ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>γ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau=1/\\gamma=2\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nStarting at mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x(0)\\rangle=3\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>e<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.10<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle x(2\\,\\mathrm s)\\rangle=3e^{-1}\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}\\approx1.10\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe figure shows the normalized stationary density.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_199_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Symmetric stationary Gaussian density of an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process with two-micrometre standard deviation.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Symmetric stationary Gaussian density of\nan Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process with two-micrometre standard\ndeviation.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Synthetic linear-noise model, not particle measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is stationary variance when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis doubled?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is relaxation time if\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>γ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\gamma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Which term represents diffusion in the Fokker–Planck equation?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>8<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">μ<\/mi><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">8\\,\\mathrm{\\mu m^2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><msubsup><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D\\partial_x^2p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The OU stationary distribution is Gaussian only under the stated\nconditions. State-dependent diffusion, nonlinear drift, absorbing\nboundaries and jump processes can have different stationary densities. A\nFokker–Planck equation requires initial and boundary conditions; zero\ncurrent is not a universal boundary choice.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>The same competition between diffusion and directed drift occurs in\nmaterial transport. <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Next\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/5-72-non-equilibrium-statistical-mechanics-spring-2012/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Non-Equilibrium Statistical Mechanics: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic index for Brownian motion, stochastic processes and the Fokker–Planck equation; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L6 – Langevin Dynamics, the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck Process and Fokker–Planck · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_physics_en:M39-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M39-L7 – Advection, Diffusion and the Péclet Number in Biological Systems<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M39 – Master: Biophysics, Nonequilibrium and Complex Systems<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · advanced specialization<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M39-L2: reaction–diffusion; M39-L6:\ndrift and noise · <a href=\"index.html?course=university_physics&amp;module=M39&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">Previous\nlesson<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish advective and\ndiffusive solute fluxes and scale their competition with the Péclet\nnumber.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Guiding question and context<\/h2>\n<p>In a microchannel, fluid flow carries dissolved material while\nmolecular diffusion smooths concentration variations along and across\nthe stream. Dominance depends on velocity, diffusivity and the chosen\nlength scale. The Péclet number is a ratio of effects, not an additional\ntransport force.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Physical model and derivation<\/h2>\n<p>For constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand incompressible flow\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe total flux is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J=\\mathbf u c-D\\nabla c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWithout reaction, concentration obeys\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∂<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>c<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mo>⋅<\/mo><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><msup><mi>∇<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>c<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\partial_tc+\\mathbf u\\cdot\\nabla c=D\\nabla^2c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith velocity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}=UL/D=\\tau_{\\mathrm{diff}}/\\tau_{\\mathrm{adv}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>U<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{adv}}=L/U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mi>L<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mi>/<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{diff}}=L^2/D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA large\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsignals advective dominance on that scale, not zero diffusion near walls\nor in boundary layers.<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝑱<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">𝒖<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><mi>D<\/mi><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbf J=\\mathbf u c-D\\nabla c,\\qquad\\mathrm{Pe}=\\frac{UL}{D}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Worked example and teaching figure<\/h2>\n<p>For illustrative microchannel parameters\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm{m\\,s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L=10^{-4}\\,\\mathrm m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">2<\/mn><\/msup><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">−<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"normal\">1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=10^{-10}\\,\\mathrm{m^2s^{-1}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nTimescales are\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{adv}}=1\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{diff}}=100\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIncreasing\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ntenfold reduces\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nto ten. The figure represents timescales and directions\nschematically.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-physics-en/media/img/U_PHY_004_EN.svg\"\nalt=\"Schematic microchannel with directed advection and cross-stream diffusion and a timescale comparison.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Schematic microchannel with directed\nadvection and cross-stream diffusion and a timescale\ncomparison.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Illustrative microchannel scale, not flow measurements.<\/em><\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Self-check<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"exercises\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">How does\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nchange when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis halved?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>τ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">v<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\tau_{\\mathrm{adv}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>U<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles?<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does diffusion still matter at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathrm{Pe}=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h3 id=\"solutions\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Solutions<\/h3>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">It falls to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>50<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.5<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5\\,\\mathrm s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Near walls and transversely, large concentration gradients can make\ndiffusion important.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limits of validity and quality<\/h2>\n<p>The scaling does not predict exact flow profiles or transport with\ncoupled reactions, turbulence or osmotic coupling. Flow fields require\nconsistent boundary conditions or measurements. The length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmust match the geometry and direction of transport.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Summary and outlook<\/h2>\n<p>M39 connects thermal noise, macroscopic fluxes, biological feedback\nand dynamical instability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S7\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources (external)<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"references\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>References<\/h3>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-430j-fields-forces-and-flows-in-biological-systems-fall-2015/pages/calendar/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Fields, Forces, and Flows in Biological Systems (Calendar)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Topic coverage for Stokes–Einstein, Fick, reaction–diffusion and Péclet number; not an independent formula check; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L7 – Advection, Diffusion and the Péclet Number in Biological Systems · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/20-430j-fields-forces-and-flows-in-biological-systems-fall-2015/resources/lecture-notes/\">MIT OpenCourseWare – Fields, Forces, and Flows: Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · Lists instructional materials on reaction, diffusion and transport; Subject relevance to lesson: M39-L7 – Advection, Diffusion and the Péclet Number in Biological Systems · Last accessed: 2026-11-21<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
